y wins in South Carolina, Oklahoma"},"12103510":{"videoId":"12103510","videoTitle":"Country music legend Dolly Parton dies at the age of 80","program":"- The Hill News Video","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"24","airDate":"1787682240","videoDuration":"96","originalVideoTitle":"Country music legend Dolly Parton dies at the age of 80"},"12103411":{"videoId":"12103411","videoTitle":"Trump: Navy cleared mines in Strait of Hormuz, new mines will be 'systematically destroyed'","program":"- The Hill News Video","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"24","airDate":"1787680200","videoDuration":"164","originalVideoTitle":"Trump: Navy cleared mines in Strait of Hormuz, new mines will be 'systematically destroyed'"},"12103365":{"videoId":"12103365","videoTitle":"Mike Lindell accuses MN Secretary of State of unlawful recount process","program":"- The Hill News Video","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"60","airDate":"1787679300","videoDuration":"174","originalVideoTitle":"Mike Lindell accuses MN Secretary of State of unlawful recount process"},"12102408":{"videoId":"12102408","videoTitle":"DC Bureau: Trade War Escalates","program":"-- FAST Only","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"30","airDate":"1787663820","videoDuration":"73","originalVideoTitle":"DC Bureau: Trade War Escalates"},"12100815":{"videoId":"12100815","videoTitle":"BREAKING: Supreme Court backs Trump's order to limit mail-in ballots ahead of midterms | NEWSNATION","program":"- The Hill News Video","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"24","airDate":"1787611800","videoDuration":"148","originalVideoTitle":"BREAKING: Supreme Court backs Trump's order to limit mail-in ballots ahead of midterms | NEWSNATION"},"12103448":{"videoId":"12103448","videoTitle":"Battleground Republicans seek distance from Trump on key issues | THE FIGHT FOR 2028","program":"- The Hill News Video","fullEpisode":true,"framerate":"24","airDate":"1787681160","videoDuration":"155","originalVideoTitle":"Battleground Republicans seek distance from Trump on key issues | THE FIGHT FOR 2028"}},"poster":"https:\/\/h104216-ucdn.mp.lura.live\/iupl_lin\/91E\/933\/91E9330AD2398B7BD5173B3DE1ED331F.jpg?aktaexp=2082787200&aktasgn=abd19d88a2405c9668bf669a459979ab","sources":[{"src":"https:\/\/tkx.mp.lura.live\/rest\/v2\/mcp\/video\/12106538.m3u8?anvack=Rawk5AaXamlYMHRoV0UeKfkdB3KQ0dnD&eud=NExlctJX0MT7X8mwrM3XdzqUHVjPM0Frsq9TRUEb4ak%2FKJgRKZ%2B64LxwkKEbw%2B0YTyO8KsbFw40vgX8hrw%2Bxqw%3D%3D","videoType":"video","renderMethod":"static"}],"title":"Poll: Data centers gain massive support -- they don't waste water! Robby Soave | RISING","error":null,"playerId":"nxsplayer_31ce05543b","adTagUrl":"https:\/\/pubads.g.doubleclick.net\/gampad\/ads?sz=1x1000&iu=\/5678\/nx.thehill\/opinion\/opinions___robby_s_radar&ciu_szs=300x250&impl=s&gdfp_req=1&env=vp&output=vmap&unviewed_position_start=1&ad_rule=1&description_url=https:\/\/thehill.com\/opinion\/robbys-radar\/6052757-data-center-water-usage-myths\/&vconp=2&cust_params=station%3DTheHill%26pid%3D6052757%26pers_cid%3Dthehill-2-article-6052757%26vidcat%3D\/opinion\/opinions___robby_s_radar%26bob_ck%3D[bob_ck_val]%26d_code%3D1%26pagetype%3Dstory%26hlmeta%3D6052757%20data%20center%20water%20usage%20myths%26aa%3Df&vid=12106538&cmsid=2594591"}"> NOW PLAYING Arguments about data centers are reaching a fever pitch,&nbsp;with&nbsp;so many Americans saying they don&rsquo;t&nbsp;want these things in their town.&nbsp; Just look at how angry people are getting. &nbsp;

But one major point of contention is based on&nbsp;an apparent&nbsp;lie:&nbsp;that data centers consume a huge amount of water. It turns out that this is simply not the case at all, and in fact, the water they do use&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;which is much less than is&nbsp;used,&nbsp;for&nbsp;example,&nbsp;for&nbsp;farming&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;is easily recycled. And importantly, when people learn this fact,&nbsp; their&nbsp;views on data centers&nbsp;changed&nbsp;rapidly .&nbsp;&nbsp;

After people are told that data centers can reuse the water, they become 31 points more supportive of building them. That suggests to me that when people say&nbsp;they&rsquo;re&nbsp;against data centers,&nbsp;they&rsquo;re&nbsp;not saying&nbsp;they&rsquo;re&nbsp;against AI or technology or anything of that nature.

What they&rsquo;re concerned about is resource depletion, and they have been told&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;in some cases by hostile foreign actors&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;that data centers are going to strain the power grid and&nbsp;use up&nbsp;all the water and pollute the rivers and make a lot of noise. None of those things are true, and the people trying to convince you they are true are far-left degrowth activists who&nbsp;don&rsquo;t&nbsp;want us to build&nbsp;anything,&nbsp;and&nbsp;would in fact like to see communist China outcompete the U.S.&nbsp;

Now,&nbsp;there is some genuine conservative opposition to the centers as well. The Federalist&rsquo;s John Daniel Davidson, for instance,&nbsp; wrote on X :&nbsp;&ldquo;Bringing in data centers to save a dying town is like bringing in casinos and&nbsp;race tracks to save a dying city.&rdquo;&nbsp;&nbsp;

That&rsquo;s&nbsp;an absurd comparison, of course. Casinos and&nbsp;race tracks&nbsp;make my money via the promotion of vice&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;which,&nbsp;by the way,&nbsp;I&rsquo;m&nbsp;perfectly fine with&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;whereas&nbsp;data centers are building the infrastructure that makes modern life&nbsp;possible,&nbsp;and&nbsp;harming absolutely no one in the process. In fact, their existence even allows local governments to lower your property taxes, which sounds like&nbsp;a pretty good&nbsp;deal to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

But also,&nbsp;if not data centers, then what is your plan to revitalize dying American towns? I keep hearing from national conservatives that they want to bring manufacturing back to the U.S.&nbsp;and build things here, but if we can&rsquo;t even build a data center because it&rsquo;s too noisy, how are we possibly going to have a domestic industrial policy?&nbsp;We&rsquo;re&nbsp;just going to end up doing nothing, and stagnating because of it. Which is exactly what our enemies want. It&rsquo;s&nbsp;what the left seems to want. It should not be what conservatives want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

To be clear,&nbsp;there&rsquo;s&nbsp;a significant contingent of MAGA that sees through this. According to Echelon Insights ,&nbsp;Republicans that are &ldquo;more conservative, male, and MAGA&rdquo; are the most supportive of data centers within the Republican coalition.&nbsp;That&rsquo;s&nbsp;not so surprising&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;President Trump himself remains entirely supportive &nbsp;of building more data centers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

When&nbsp;he&rsquo;s&nbsp;right,&nbsp;he&rsquo;s&nbsp;right. Data centers create jobs and prosperity, and the amount of water they use is a drop in the bucket. Tell your friends and neighbors&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;spread the word!&nbsp;&nbsp;

Robby Soave is co-host of The Hill&rsquo;s commentary show &ldquo;Rising&rdquo; and a senior editor for Reason Magazine. This column is an edited transcription of his daily commentary. &nbsp;

